---
// DESIGN · terrain-journey — the documentation page for the journeys package.
//
// This is the page to read before building the next interactive map feature.
// It documents the contract, the recipe, and the mistakes that cost real time —
// the last of which is the part that actually saves anyone anything.
//
// The package itself lives in packages/terrain-journey and knows nothing about
// this site; two features consume it today.
import DesignShell from '../../components/design/DesignShell.astro';

import { churam } from '../../lib/journeys/churam';
import { chaliyar } from '../../lib/journeys/chaliyar';

/** Live figures, read off the journeys rather than typed here. */
const built = [
  {
    href: '/features/thamarassery-churam',
    name: 'Thamarassery Churam',
    kind: 'road',
    j: churam,
    views: 'Chase / Driver',
  },
  {
    href: '/features/rivers-of-kerala',
    name: 'The Chaliyar',
    kind: 'river',
    j: chaliyar,
    views: 'Above / On the water',
  },
];

const contract = `interface Journey {
  id: string;
  bbox: [west, south, east, north];
  route: [lng, lat, metresASL, metresFromStart][];
  markers: Marker[];      // hairpins, towns, confluences
  places: Place[];        // named, labelled, beside the line
  ribbon: { kind: 'road' | 'river'; width; lift; tubeRadius };
  verticalScale: { overview: number; firstPerson: number };
  length: number; lowest: number; highest: number;

  palette?: Partial<Palette>;      // colours by role
  scenery?: Partial<Scenery>;      // facets, trees
  camera?: Partial<CameraSpec>;    // per-journey framing
  markerRadius?: number;
}`;

const usage = `---
import JourneyStage from '../../components/journey/JourneyStage.astro';
import { chaliyar } from '../../lib/journeys/chaliyar';
---

<JourneyStage
  journey={chaliyar}
  overviewLabel="Above"
  firstPersonLabel="On the water"
  playLabel="Flow"
  markerNoun="Town"
/>`;

/** The two views, and why each number pulls the opposite way. */
const disagreements = [
  {
    what: 'Look-ahead',
    overview: 'Long — hundreds of metres',
    first: 'Short — tens of metres',
    why:
      'Aimed down the instantaneous tangent the overview whips through 170° at every hairpin, and hides the very thing worth showing. Averaged, the heading barely moves while the traveller sweeps the turn beneath it, so the bend unrolls as a shape. First person wants the opposite: the turning is the experience. Set long, its aim point at a hairpin apex sits most of the way back round the loop — so the camera stares across the gap at the road it just left.',
  },
  {
    what: 'Position smoothing',
    overview: 'Damped',
    first: 'None — exact every frame',
    why:
      'A damped first-person position looks harmless on a straight, then cuts the corner at a hairpin and puts the viewpoint out over the drop. Only the aim is damped, and that is what supplies the feeling of steering into a bend rather than snapping to it.',
  },
  {
    what: 'Vertical scale',
    overview: 'Exaggerated',
    first: 'True',
    why:
      'Geometry is built at true scale; exaggeration is a Y scale on the group holding it, so the views disagree without rebuilding a vertex. From height, real relief is a gentle ramp and exaggeration makes it legible. From inside, that same factor doubles every gradient the body reads. Whichever is in force is stated on screen.',
  },
];

/** Each of these was a bug, found by looking rather than by reasoning. */
const traps = [
  [
    'Reading a WebGL canvas with drawImage returns black',
    'Without preserveDrawingBuffer the buffer is gone by the time you read it. It looks exactly like a scene failing to render. Screenshot the page instead.',
  ],
  [
    'Eye height is measured from the surface, not the route',
    'The ribbon sits above the route. Measuring from the route put the viewpoint 0.7 m over the tarmac, and the road filled the lower half of the screen.',
  ],
  [
    'The line must ride over whichever surface is higher',
    'The route carries per-point DEM samples; the terrain mesh is a coarser grid over the same DEM and averages a valley floor upward. Drawn at its own elevation the line runs underneath its own landscape — a 105 km river was invisible from directly above it.',
  ],
  [
    'Clear the sightline, not the ground',
    'Keeping the camera above the ground underfoot lets it burrow into a hillside. Keeping it above the highest point between it and its target still fails, because the ray dips back into that ridge. Solve the ray equation — but only over the near two-thirds, or the 1/(1−t) term multiplies the margin twentyfold and flings the camera kilometres up.',
  ],
  [
    'Aim the clearance at the traveller, not the aim point',
    'The aim sits further down the line, so its ray is shallower. Clearing it leaves the steeper ray to the traveller cutting straight through the ridge.',
  ],
  [
    'Controls cannot live inside the stage',
    'A position:fixed element at z-index 0 is its own stacking context, so nothing inside it rises above z-index 10 page content and the buttons are unclickable. The stage is aria-hidden too, which is no place for interactive controls.',
  ],
  [
    'Zoom, facets and tree spacing scale with the bbox',
    'A 6 km ghat needs six DEM tiles at zoom 13; a 105 km river covers the same ground in 143. Zoom is chosen against a tile budget and facet size follows it down — detail finer than the DEM carries buys triangles and nothing else.',
  ],
  [
    'Cones read as spruce',
    'Which puts an alpine forest on a tropical ghat. A faceted icosahedron is truer for broadleaf canopy, and canopy should be about half as wide as the tree is tall — wider turns every tree into a hedge.',
  ],
];
---

<DesignShell
  title="terrain-journey — scroll-driven 3D journeys | MediaOne Design"
  description="The contract, recipe and pitfalls for building interactive terrain features."
  eyebrow="Package"
  heading="terrain-journey"
  lede="Scroll-driven 3D journeys over real terrain. Give it a line through the world — a road, a river, a trek — with real coordinates and elevations, and it renders a low-poly landscape you can travel along two ways."
  active="components"
>
  <!-- ══ WHAT IS BUILT ════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="article-title text-text-primary dark:text-ivory">Built on it</h2>
    <div class="mt-5 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {built.map((b) => (
        <a
          href={b.href}
          class="group block border border-rule p-5 transition-colors hover:border-azure dark:border-ivory/12 dark:hover:border-azure-light"
        >
          <p class="eyebrow text-azure dark:text-azure-light">{b.kind}</p>
          <h3 class="mt-2 article-title text-text-primary group-hover:text-azure dark:text-ivory dark:group-hover:text-azure-light">
            {b.name}
          </h3>
          <dl class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2 caption-text tabular-nums text-text-muted dark:text-ivory/50">
            <div><dt class="inline">Length </dt><dd class="inline text-text-primary dark:text-ivory/80">{(b.j.length / 1000).toFixed(1)} km</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="inline">Route </dt><dd class="inline text-text-primary dark:text-ivory/80">{b.j.route.length} pts</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="inline">Markers </dt><dd class="inline text-text-primary dark:text-ivory/80">{b.j.markers.length}</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="inline">Rise </dt><dd class="inline text-text-primary dark:text-ivory/80">{b.j.highest - b.j.lowest} m</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p class="mt-3 caption-text text-text-light dark:text-ivory/35">{b.views}</p>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ THE RULE ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="mt-14 border-l-2 border-azure pl-5 dark:border-azure-light">
    <h2 class="article-title text-text-primary dark:text-ivory">
      Geography is derived, not recalled
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl body-small text-text-muted dark:text-ivory/60 text-pretty">
      Coordinates, names and elevations come from OpenStreetMap and a DEM, through
      a build script. This is not a style preference. A first draft of one journey
      was typed from memory and put a village four kilometres from where it is, on
      a ridge, a thousand metres above the valley floor it actually sits on.
      Another dataset labelled a river's <em>headwater vertex</em> with the name of
      a town 25 km downstream. Both looked entirely plausible on screen.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl body-small font-semibold text-text-primary dark:text-ivory/80">
      If a figure cannot be traced to a query, it does not go in.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ CONTRACT ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="mt-14">
    <h2 class="article-title text-text-primary dark:text-ivory">The contract</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl body-small text-text-muted dark:text-ivory/55">
      One shape describes a road climbing a ghat and a river running to the sea.
      Everything after <code>highest</code> is optional.
    </p>
    <pre class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto border border-rule bg-bg-page p-5 text-[0.75rem] leading-[1.7] text-text-primary dark:border-ivory/12 dark:bg-brand-dark dark:text-ivory/80"><code>{contract}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ USAGE ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="article-title text-text-primary dark:text-ivory">Using it here</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl body-small text-text-muted dark:text-ivory/55">
      <code>JourneyStage.astro</code> owns the markup, styling and controls; the
      package owns the terrain, the camera and the loop. A new feature is a data
      file and this tag.
    </p>
    <pre class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto border border-rule bg-bg-page p-5 text-[0.75rem] leading-[1.7] text-text-primary dark:border-ivory/12 dark:bg-brand-dark dark:text-ivory/80"><code>{usage}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ THE TWO VIEWS ════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="mt-14">
    <h2 class="article-title text-text-primary dark:text-ivory">
      The two views disagree on purpose
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-6">
      {disagreements.map((d) => (
        <div class="border-t border-rule pt-4 dark:border-ivory/12">
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-6 gap-y-1">
            <h3 class="article-title text-text-primary dark:text-ivory">{d.what}</h3>
            <p class="caption-text text-text-muted dark:text-ivory/50">
              overview: <span class="text-azure dark:text-azure-light">{d.overview}</span>
              <span class="mx-2 text-text-light dark:text-ivory/25">·</span>
              first person: <span class="text-azure dark:text-azure-light">{d.first}</span>
            </p>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl body-small text-text-muted dark:text-ivory/55 text-pretty">{d.why}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ THE REVEAL CARD ══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="mt-14">
    <h2 class="article-title text-text-primary dark:text-ivory">The reveal card</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl body-small text-text-muted dark:text-ivory/55 text-pretty">
      A marker carrying a <code>title</code> gets a card that appears as the
      traveller reaches it, holds while it is the nearest marker, and hands over
      to the next — so on a long journey there is always something on screen
      naming where you are.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl body-small text-text-muted dark:text-ivory/55 text-pretty">
      Sticky, but <strong>positionally aware</strong>. It moves to whichever side
      the place is actually on so it never covers it, tracks the place's height,
      and points at it with a caret. When the place goes behind the camera the
      caret swings round rather than the card vanishing — <em>you have just
      passed it</em> is information too. Markers with no title get no card; a
      hairpin's number already says everything about it.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl body-small text-text-primary dark:text-ivory/75 text-pretty">
      <code>note</code> is the one field in the format where a fabrication would
      be least visible. Only ever write there what a source actually says. Six of
      the Chaliyar's ten towns have no note, and the card is better for it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ TRAPS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="mt-14">
    <h2 class="article-title text-text-primary dark:text-ivory">
      Things that cost real time
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl body-small text-text-muted dark:text-ivory/55">
      Each of these was a bug, and each was found by looking at the screen rather
      than by reasoning about the code. This list is the most useful thing on this
      page.
    </p>
    <ol class="mt-6 space-y-5">
      {traps.map(([title, body], i) => (
        <li class="grid gap-2 border-t border-rule pt-4 sm:grid-cols-[2rem_1fr] dark:border-ivory/12">
          <span class="caption-text tabular-nums text-text-light dark:text-ivory/30">
            {String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}
          </span>
          <div>
            <h3 class="article-title text-text-primary dark:text-ivory">{title}</h3>
            <p class="mt-1.5 max-w-3xl body-small text-text-muted dark:text-ivory/55 text-pretty">{body}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ RECIPE ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="mt-14">
    <h2 class="article-title text-text-primary dark:text-ivory">Building the next one</h2>
    <ol class="mt-5 max-w-3xl list-decimal space-y-3 pl-5 body-small text-text-muted dark:text-ivory/60 marker:text-azure dark:marker:text-azure-light">
      <li>Query the line from OSM via Overpass, stitched and oriented in travel order.</li>
      <li>Sample a DEM per point. Thin to a few hundred, keeping every marker apex.</li>
      <li>Look up places. <strong>Never</strong> reuse rounded coordinates from another dataset.</li>
      <li>Emit a module exporting a <code>Journey</code>, with a header recording every source and every judgement call.</li>
      <li>Verify: markers land where the source says, the line is visible in both views, reduced motion holds one frame, and the server-rendered fallback lists everything.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl body-small text-text-muted dark:text-ivory/60 text-pretty">
      Detection thresholds must be <strong>calibrated against known anchors, not
      tuned until they agree with a number you already believed</strong>. On the
      churam, OSM names only two of the nine hairpins; every parameter set that
      placed those two at ordinals 7 and 8 was kept, and nine was the majority
      outcome of a constraint that never mentioned nine.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ STATUS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="mt-14 border border-rule p-5 dark:border-ivory/12">
    <h2 class="article-title text-text-primary dark:text-ivory">Status — 0.1.0, in-repo</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 max-w-3xl list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 body-small text-text-muted dark:text-ivory/55">
      <li>
        <strong>Ships TypeScript source, not a build.</strong> Fine for a workspace
        consumed by Vite; a compile step is needed before it is usable from plain
        JS or publishable to npm.
      </li>
      <li>No tests of its own — verified through the two features that consume it.</li>
      <li>No terrain occlusion for labels: one behind a ridge still shows.</li>
      <li>Checked at 1440×900 only.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</DesignShell>
